<h2>Indian spices for export</h2>
<p>India is the world's largest producer, consumer and exporter of spices, and the export trade is bigger than most buyers realise — dried red chilli alone was a USD 1.3 billion export line in 2024, with <a href="/markets/china/">China</a> taking roughly a third of it.</p>

<p>We supply turmeric, dried red chilli, cumin, coriander, black pepper and cardamom, whole or processed, machine-cleaned and sortex-sorted, from the growing belts that specialise in each.</p>

<h2>Variety matters more than most buyers expect</h2>
<p>The single biggest source of disappointment on Indian spice orders is under-specification. "Red chilli" is not a specification — Guntur Sannam, Byadgi and Teja are three genuinely different products:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Guntur (Sannam / S4)</strong> — the volume workhorse, good colour and moderate-to-high heat.</li>
  <li><strong>Byadgi</strong> — prized for deep red colour with relatively low pungency; used where colour is the objective (including oleoresin extraction).</li>
  <li><strong>Teja</strong> — very high pungency, used where heat is the objective.</li>
</ul>
<p>The same applies across the category. Turmeric from Salem, Erode and Nizamabad differs in curcumin content. Cardamom is graded by size and colour. Specify what you actually need.</p>

<h2>How spices are specified for export</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Variety and origin belt</strong> — as above.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture</strong> — critical for storage stability and for avoiding mould.</li>
  <li><strong>Purity / admixture</strong> — machine-cleaned and sortex-sorted grades.</li>
  <li><strong>Colour value (ASTA)</strong> — for chilli and paprika-type products.</li>
  <li><strong>Pungency (SHU)</strong> — for chilli.</li>
  <li><strong>Curcumin content</strong> — for turmeric.</li>
  <li><strong>Volatile oil</strong> — for cumin, coriander, cardamom and pepper.</li>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — whole, crushed, ground; and whether steam sterilisation is required.</li>
</ul>

